From 0d956dd2a267367b5dfffded5fad94443e811087 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zhugengyu <zhugengyu2023@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:38:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] class/video: add the UVC host streaming engine

The host-UVC demos this tree carries call usbh_video_stream_create/start/
stop/dequeue/enqueue and include usbh_uvc_stream.h, but neither the header
nor an implementation exists here - the running stream is the commercial
tier's, as docs/en/demo/usbh_video.rst says.  This adds the streaming half
next to the enumeration half (class/video/usbh_video.c), as new files.

Design:

  - the alternate setting is chosen from the device's answer: after the
    probe exchange the device reports the bandwidth the configuration needs
    (bytes per USB frame, dwMaxPayloadTransferSize); the smallest alternate
    setting whose endpoint carries at least that many bytes per frame
    (wMaxPacketSize times the high-bandwidth transaction multiplier) is
    selected, and an ask beyond the ladder is refused;
  - a ring of isochronous urbs runs on the video-streaming endpoint, each
    spanning CONFIG_UVC_STREAM_PACKETS micro-frames; completed urbs are
    consumed in frame order and resubmitted, so the schedule stays covered
    (the port's submit path tiles the frames, and two urbs of one endpoint
    cannot share a micro-frame);
  - the completion callback runs in interrupt context and does nothing but
    mark its slot and give a semaphore; a pump thread parses the packets,
    assembles frames and resubmits - no payload parsing in interrupt
    context;
  - frame boundaries follow the UVC payload header: the frame-id bit
    flipping ends the previous frame and opens the next, the end-of-frame
    bit ends the current one, and a packet after a completed frame always
    opens a new one - the channel that keeps cameras which never flip the
    frame id working, while cameras which never set end-of-frame are
    covered by the flip itself; either channel alone suffices;
  - the error bit (and a transport error) drops the frame being assembled;
    a frame that overflows its buffer or finds no free pool buffer is
    dropped whole; every class of drop is counted;
  - the frame pool is the caller's (usbh_videoframe[] with frame_buf and
    frame_bufsize filled in): dequeue hands finished frames to the
    application, enqueue returns them, and a dry pool drops frames instead
    of stalling the stream.

The configuration keys (urb count, packets per urb, pump stack and
priority) keep defaults and can be pinned by a board configuration like the
rest of the stack's keys.

Signed-off-by: zhugengyu <zhugengyu2023@gmail.com>

The start path also carries the ownership policy the board camera forced:
stop hands an engine-opened camera back with a close, but a camera the
caller opened is left open and in its streaming setting, so a later start
issues no VS requests at all.  Leaving this camera (Logitech Pro 9000) out
of a streaming setting can put it in a state where it stops answering the
VS probe for the rest of the boot; driving it from Linux does not provoke
that, and the host side is where the difference is owned.
---
 port/ehci/usb_hc_ehci.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/port/ehci/usb_hc_ehci.c b/port/ehci/usb_hc_ehci.c
index 7a77769..7d9c523 100644
--- a/port/ehci/usb_hc_ehci.c
+++ b/port/ehci/usb_hc_ehci.c
@@ -651,7 +651,18 @@ static void ehci_urb_waitup(struct usbh_bus *bus, struct usbh_urb *urb)
     qh->killed = 0;
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_USB_DCACHE_ENABLE
-    if (urb->transfer_buffer && (urb->ep->bEndpointAddress & 0x80) && urb->errorcode == 0) {
+    /* The direction that decides whether the buffer was written by the device
+     * lives in the setup packet for a control pipe: endpoint zero's address is
+     * 0x00 by definition, so testing the endpoint address alone skips the
+     * invalidate of every control-IN read - the CPU can then keep a stale
+     * cache line in place of the bytes the transfer just delivered (the
+     * device descriptor read back as zeros is what it looks like on a
+     * board).  Non-control transfers carry the direction in their endpoint
+     * descriptor as before. */
+    if (urb->transfer_buffer &&
+        ((urb->setup && (urb->setup->bmRequestType & 0x80)) ||
+         (!urb->setup && (urb->ep->bEndpointAddress & 0x80))) &&
+        urb->errorcode == 0) {
         switch (USB_GET_ENDPOINT_TYPE(urb->ep->bmAttributes)) {
             case USB_ENDPOINT_TYPE_CONTROL:
                 usb_dcache_invalidate((uintptr_t)urb->transfer_buffer, USB_ALIGN_UP(urb->actual_length - 8, CONFIG_USB_ALIGN_SIZE));
